  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nQuiet and dry weather expected through Thanksgiving morning. It will \nbe much cooler, with highs mainly in the lower 30s to lower 40s \nduring the afternoon. We could see widespread lows in the teens\neach morning which would be the coldest of the season thus far.\nWill then see deteriorating conditions Thanksgiving afternoon \nwith the brunt of the impacts overnight into Black Friday \nmorning.\n\nA coastal low will be developing east of FL on Wednesday night \nwithin a southern stream shortwave trough before moving \nnorthward towards the Carolinas on Thursday. As it moves up the \ncoast, it will interact with an upper-level low over the Ohio \nValley. These features are not expected to phase but rather \nremain as two separate entities, with the surface low tracking \ninto the Gulf of Maine by early Friday, bringing the majority of\nthe heavy rain and impacts to the region. Rain will overspread \nfrom south to north Thanksgiving evening, quickly becoming \nheavy. Expecting a quick 0.5-1.5\", likely falling within a six \nhour period or less. The upper-level low will lag behind, \nbringing lingering precipitation into Friday, which will be \nshowery and much lighter in accumulation.\n\nThe lower elevations/coastal plain should be all rain for the \ninitial thump Thursday night, however continuing to see a decent\ncold air damming signal for the interior as some near-surface \ncold air gets jammed up against the mountains. With 850 mb \ntemperatures hovering around +3 to +6 C at the onset of \nprecipitation, not expecting sleet/snow at this point. Will have\nto watch closely for a period of freezing rain in the interior \nif temperatures can remain below freezing; right now it looks \nquite marginal. Chances for light snow showers will return by \nFriday afternoon as the airmass aloft cools behind the cold \nfront, initially for the mountains, but some flakes for the \nlower elevations may be possible by Friday night-Saturday \nmorning.\n\nAs for wind, model soundings indicate mixing up to about 925 mb\nwith a steep inversion above that for the coastal plain. Winds \nat 925 mb are around 45 kts, so reasonably think we could mix \nabout 70% of that, resulting in gusts up around 35 mph along the\ncoast. A pretty thick inversion will cap winds by the shore, \nwhile further inland, an even more shallow near-surface mixing \nprofile should result in even weaker winds outside of the \nmountain peaks.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA trend for earlier arrival of the storm occlusion means two \nhigh tide cycles could reach minor flood stage for coastal \nflooding. \n\nThe first around midnight Thursday night will feature the lower\ntide of the two, but may coincide with greater surge and wave \naction. Should timing align, these factors would make up for the\nlower background water levels, nearing Minor flood stage from \nHampton up towards Portland. With a larger ensemble uncertainty \nthan the following tide, will watch for more agreement in timing\nto further increase confidence for minor flooding and splash-\nover.\n\nThe following high tide around noontime Friday will feature the\nhigher astronomical tide. However the strong onshore wind will \nhave lifted northeast with slowly decreasing wave action. Even \nstill, surge guidance keeps around a foot of surge along the \nNH/ME coasts, enough to lead to more widespread Minor coastal \nflooding threat including towards the Midcoast.\n\nWould expect strong breaking waves to bring beach erosion \nthrough these tides. Wave heights in the near coastal waters \nwill quickly rise to 7-9ft Thursday evening, to 10-14ft around \nmidnight. Wave heights slowly decrease through Friday morning, \nbut still remain in the 7-10ft range.",
